2.1.4 Representing Strings

A string in C is encoded by an array of characters terminated by the null (having value 0) character. Each 
character is represented by some standard encoding, with the most common being the ASCII character code. Thus, if 
we run our routine show_bytes with arguments “12345” and 6 (to include the terminating character), we get the 
result 31 32 33 34 35 00. Observe that the ASCII code for decimal digit x happens to be 0x3x, and that the 
terminating byte has the hex representation 0x00. This same result would be obtained on any system using ASCII as 
its character code, independent of the byte ordering and word size conventions. As a consequence, text data are 
more platform independent than binary data.

