Conversions between Signed and Unsigned

For most implementations of C, the answer to this question is based on a bit-level perspective, rather than on a numeric one. For most C implementations that handle conversions between signed and unsigned numbers with the same word size—the numeric values might change, but the bit patterns do not. For example, consider the following code:

1  short	int	 v = –12345;
2  unsigned short uv = (unsigned short) v;
3  printf(“v = %d, uv = %u\n”, v, uv);

When run on a two's-complement machine, it generates the following output:

v = –12345, uv = 53191

What we see here is that the effect of casting is to keep the bit values identical but change how these bits are interpreted. Similarly, consider the following code:

1  unsigned u = 4294967295u;	/* UMax */
2  int tu = (int) u;
3  printf(“u = %u, tu = %d\n”, u, tu);

When run on a two's-complement machine, it generates the following output:

u = 4294967295, tu = –1

This is a general rule for how most C implementations handle conversions between signed and unsigned numbers with the same word size—the numeric values might change, but the bit patterns do not. see equation2-5.png and equation2-7

