Truncating Numbers

Suppose that, rather than extending a value with extra bits, we reduce the number of bits representing a number.

say you have 

int x = 53191

using bc <<< "obase=2;ibase=10;53191" you know that the bit representation is

00000000000000001100111111000111

short sx = (short) x;	/* -12345 */”

will take the lower 16 bits (thereby discarding the upper 16 bits) and place them into the short data type which is 16 bits wide. Since the data type is declared as signed, i know that the number will be negative since the msb is 1. to find out what number it is in decimal, use the following one liner in python

python3 -c 'b="1100111111000111"; n=int(b,2); bits=len(b); print(n - (1<<bits) if b[0]=="1" else n)'

this statement

int y = sx;     /* –12345 */

places the 16 bits into a wider data type (32 bits) and sign extends the msb to fill the upper (intially empty) 16 bits. the end result is that the y stores the same number as variable sx.

Advice on Signed versus Unsigned

As we have seen, the implicit casting of signed to unsigned leads to some nonintuitive behavior. Nonintuitive features often lead to program bugs, and ones involving the nuances of implicit casting can be especially difficult to see. Since the casting takes place without any clear indication in the code, programmers often overlook its effects.


